   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.15",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Limits at Infinity and Horizontal Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = (3x + 2)/(x − 4), where x ≠ 4. Which line is a horizontal asymptote of the graph of f?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "y = 0",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Denominator-only reasoning: a growing denominator does not force the quotient toward 0 when the numerator also grows. Here, the numerator and denominator have the same degree. Fix: Divide both by x and evaluate the limit of the entire quotient."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "y = 3",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Dividing the numerator and denominator by x gives f(x) = (3 + 2/x)/(1 − 4/x). As x → ∞ or x → −∞, the terms 2/x and 4/x approach 0, so f(x) approaches 3. Therefore, y = 3 is a horizontal asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "y = −1/2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Finite-input substitution error: substituting x = 0 gives f(0) = −1/2, not the end behavior. A horizontal asymptote depends on a limit as x → ∞ or x → −∞. Fix: Let x approach infinity instead of substituting a finite input."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "x = 4",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Vertical–horizontal asymptote confusion: setting the denominator equal to 0 identifies the vertical asymptote here. The line x = 4 is vertical, so it cannot be a horizontal asymptote. Fix: Compute an end-behavior limit, then write the horizontal line as y = L."
     }
    ]
   },
